# FIXED DEFECT 032, 2026-09-14 (M-cleanup-verdict step 1), found while running
# this milestone's opening census — a probe that walked SQLite's own list of
# open statements answered `0` where the same program in C answered `1`.
#
# SYMPTOM: `==` on a handle was a constant `true`, at exit 0, in silence. Five
# questions, four wrong answers:
#
#     a == nullptr (want false): true
#     a != nullptr (want true):  false
#     a == b       (want false): true
#     a == a       (want true):  true
#     null == null (want true):  true
#
# and the wrong answer travelled through every container that asks a handle's
# `_eq` — a Heroes record holding one, an array of them, a `Chunk?`.
#
# WHAT IT BREAKS is the commonest line in a binding. `if h == nullptr` after an
# init that can fail was always taken, and `if h != nullptr` never was — so a
# guard written the second way let a null handle through to the next C call.
# spec § 13 promises the opposite in as many words: *`nullptr` is its null and
# `==` compares the address*, and `handles.handle_map_key`'s note tells the
# programmer *two handles are equal when their addresses are*. The compiler
# stated the rule in a diagnostic and broke it in the C it generated.
#
# CAUSE: `selfhost/emit/structural.hero`'s zero-field arm emitted
# `(void)a; (void)b; return true;` under the comment *record E is
# error[empty_record] in the checker, so this is belt to those braces*. That is
# a premise about the WORLD and it was true when written; M-handle-verdict
# killed it on 2026-09-14 by making a fieldless group `record` with a `tag`
# legal and giving it a pointer C type. The premise died, the comment went on
# reading as correct, and the fallback had been put in the QUIET direction —
# the one thing `.claude/rules/module-shape.md` says not to do, fifteen lines
# below a `partial` arm in the same function that gets it right with
# `hero_panic`.
#
# WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT: `fixedbugs-a-handle-in-a-record-field.hero`, landed the
# day before, asserts `print(s.handle == nullptr)` against an expected `true` —
# and its handle IS null, so the wrong answer and the right one are the same
# value there. The one golden that compared a handle picked the single case
# where a constant `true` is indistinguishable from an address comparison. This
# case exists to separate them.
#
# FIX: the arm asks `at.handle`, carried from `handles.is_handle_record` through
# `ctype.Aggregate`, and emits `return *a == *b;`. A fieldless record that is
# NOT a handle cannot be written at all, so that arm became `hero_panic` — the
# loud direction its neighbour already used. `_hash` folds the same address, so
# the two walks this module exists to keep together say the same thing.

extern "fixedbugs-a-handle-compares-as-an-address.h"
    record Chunk tag Chunk
    function chunk_at(i: i64) -> Chunk
    function chunk_none() -> Chunk
    function chunk_tag(c: Chunk) -> i64

# A handle as the field of a plain Heroes record, which reaches the same `_eq`
# one level down.
record Held
    h: Chunk
    name: str

function main()
    a = chunk_at(i: 0)
    b = chunk_at(i: 1)
    n = chunk_none()

    # The handles are real and distinct, so the comparisons below are asking
    # about two different addresses rather than two copies of one.
    print(chunk_tag(c: a))
    print(chunk_tag(c: b))

    # The witness: the four answers a constant `true` gets wrong, and the one
    # it gets right.
    print(a == n)
    print(a != n)
    print(a == b)
    print(a == a)
    print(n == nullptr)

    # The neighbouring shapes, each of which asks the handle's own `_eq`.
    print(Held(h: a, name: "x") == Held(h: b, name: "x"))
    print(Held(h: a, name: "x") == Held(h: a, name: "x"))
    print([a] == [b])
    print([a] == [a])
    oa: Chunk? = ok(a)
    ob: Chunk? = ok(b)
    print(oa == ob)
